<Hub pillarId="scams-and-deepfakes">
  <p>Your ear is not the test. The number already saved in your phone is. The steps below stay plain on purpose.</p>
  <h2>Can a scam use a real person’s voice?</h2>
  <p>
    Yes. The <a href="https://internationalaisafetyreport.org/sites/default/files/2026-02/international-ai-safety-report-2026.pdf">International AI Safety Report 2026</a> says scammers use AI tools to generate voice clones or deepfakes to trick people into transferring money. It describes documented cases of executives authorising large transfers, and of ordinary people sending money to someone posing as a loved one. The report says systematic data on how often this happens is still limited, so we will not invent a count.
  </p>
  <p>
    The report’s glossary defines a deepfake as AI-generated audio or visual content that depicts people saying or doing things they did not say or do, or events that did not occur. It also reports a study in which listeners took AI voice clones for the real speaker in 80 percent of cases, and a study in which warning labels on videos raised identification accuracy only from 10.7 percent to 21.6 percent. Most people in that second study still missed the fake. Your ear is a weak test.
  </p>
  <h2>What does the phone rule say?</h2>
  <p>
    In a ruling released on 8 February 2024, the <a href="https://docs.fcc.gov/public/attachments/FCC-24-17A1.pdf">FCC</a> confirmed that the Telephone Consumer Protection Act’s limits on an “artificial or prerecorded voice” cover current AI technologies that generate human voices. Calls that use those technologies need the called person’s prior express consent, unless an emergency purpose or an exemption applies. That is a rule for callers. It does not mean you can spot a clone by listening.
  </p>
  <p>
    The practical check is on the guide: hang up, and call the person back on a number you already had. Report internet crime to the FBI’s <a href="https://www.ic3.gov/">Internet Crime Complaint Center</a>, and fraud to the FTC at <a href="https://reportfraud.ftc.gov/">ReportFraud.ftc.gov</a>.
  </p>
    <p>Read <a href="/articles/ai-scams-voice-clones-and-deepfakes/">AI scams: voice clones and deepfakes</a>.</p>
